s25_extras = [
    ("Quina Retouch Characteristic", "In Middle Paleolithic Mousterian typology, what is 'Quina Retouch' (scalar retouch)?",
     "Steep, stepped, overlapping scalar flaking applied to the edges of thick scrapers, resembling fish scales",
     ["Drilling circular holes through stone axes", "Polishing stone with sand and water", "Melting stone edges with fire"],
     "Quina retouch features heavy, stepped, overlapping scalar flake scars on thick side-scrapers (racloirs), typical of the Charentian Mousterian facies."),
    ("La Chapelle-aux-Saints 'Old Man' Burial", "The nearly complete Neanderthal skeleton discovered in 1908 in a shallow grave at La Chapelle-aux-Saints (France) proved compassionate social care because:",
     "The elderly individual suffered from severe crippling osteoarthritis and had lost all his molars, surviving for years only through group provisioning and food preparation",
     ["He had two iron artificial legs fitted by doctors", "He was buried inside a golden sarcophagus", "He was found frozen in a modern refrigerator"],
     "The 'Old Man' of La Chapelle-aux-Saints had extensive arthritis and lost all teeth; he could only survive if his social group looked after him and softened his food."),
    ("Teshik-Tash Neanderthal Child Burial", "At Teshik-Tash cave in Uzbekistan, an intentional Neanderthal burial of an 8-to-9-year-old child was excavated by A.P. Okladnikov, notable for:",
     "The skeleton being encircled by five pairs of Siberian mountain goat (ibex) horns driven points-downward into the grave soil",
     ["The child being buried with an iron bicycle", "The child wearing a Roman gold coin necklace", "The skeleton being buried inside a wooden boat"],
     "The Teshik-Tash child grave was ringed by arranged ibex horns, providing unequivocal evidence of deliberate ritual funerary arrangements among Central Asian Neanderthals."),
    ("Composite Tools in Middle Paleolithic", "What technological innovation in hunting weaponry appeared during the Middle Paleolithic / Middle Stone Age?",
     "Hafting stone points onto wooden spear shafts using cordage and mastic resin, creating lethal composite thrusting spears",
     ["Automatic machine guns firing lead bullets", "Crossbows equipped with telescopic scopes", "Metal cannonballs fired with black powder"],
     "Neanderthals and MSA humans invented composite weapons: hafting triangular Levallois/Mousterian points onto wooden spears for close-quarters big game hunting."),
    ("Middle Paleolithic Big-Game Hunting Prowess", "Zooarchaeological and stable nitrogen isotope analyses have demonstrated that European Neanderthals were:",
     "Top-level apex carnivores who systematically hunted prime-age large herbivores (such as bison, wild horses, red deer, and mammoths)",
     ["Strict vegetarians who consumed only berries and bark", "Scavengers who ate only insects and dead fish", "Farmers who grew extensive wheat fields"],
     "Stable isotope ratios (Delta N-15) prove Neanderthals were hyper-carnivorous apex predators, hunting dangerous prime-age megafauna at close range with heavy thrusting spears."),
    ("Divje Babe Cave 'Neanderthal Flute'", "A 43,000-year-old cave bear femur fragment with spaced circular perforations found in 1995 at Divje Babe cave in Slovenia was claimed by some researchers to be:",
     "The world's oldest musical instrument (a Neanderthal bone flute)",
     ["A piece of an ancient steam locomotive", "A dental tool used for filling cavities", "A royal walking cane carried by an emperor"],
     "The Divje Babe bone was interpreted by Turk as a Neanderthal flute; though contested by taphonomists as carnivore puncture marks, it sparked intense debate on Neanderthal music."),
    ("Middle Stone Age (MSA) of Africa", "While Europe was dominated by Neanderthal Mousterian industries, contemporaneous anatomically modern humans in Africa (300 to 50 ka) developed the:",
     "Middle Stone Age (MSA), featuring early bladelets, bone points, engraved ochre, and shell beads (e.g., Blombos Cave)",
     ["Acheulean handaxe culture with zero change", "Industrial Revolution with steam factories", "Bronze Age megalithic tombs"],
     "In Africa, the Middle Stone Age (MSA) witnessed the earliest behavioral modernity among Homo sapiens: Still Bay and Howiesons Poort microliths, ochre engraving, and shell jewelry."),
    ("Bhimbetka Middle Paleolithic Strata", "At Bhimbetka, Middle Paleolithic stone tool assemblages directly succeed the Acheulean layers, characterized by:",
     "A notable reduction in tool size, disappearance of large cleavers, and dominance of smaller scrapers, points, and Levallois flakes made of fine quartzite and chert",
     ["Massive bronze statues of Indian deities", "Polished iron axes and swords", "Fired ceramic plates painted in gold"],
     "Bhimbetka's Middle Paleolithic shows uninterrupted technological evolution: handaxes disappear, replaced by smaller Levallois flakes, discoid cores, and scrapers."),
    ("Chatelperronian Culture Transitional Status", "In Western Europe (~45,000 to 40,000 BP), the 'Châtelperronian' culture (famous for curved-back flint knives and pierced animal teeth) is historically significant because:",
     "It represents a transitional industry manufactured by the last surviving Neanderthals just prior to their replacement by modern Aurignacian Homo sapiens",
     ["It was the culture that built Stonehenge", "It was an alien culture from outer space", "It was manufactured exclusively by modern chimpanzees"],
     "Skeletal remains at Saint-Césaire and Grotte du Renne proved Châtelperronian was made by Neanderthals, showing they adopted bone tools and body ornaments during contact with moderns."),
    ("Levallois Point Morphology", "A 'Levallois Point' is a specialized flake tool produced by the Levallois technique that features:",
     "A sharp, pointed triangular outline with a central longitudinal ridge, requiring minimal secondary retouch to function as a spearhead",
     ["A perfectly round spherical stone ball", "A hollow copper cylinder", "A square flat grinding stone"],
     "A Levallois point is detached from a convergent Levallois core: triangular, thin, and sharp, with a dorsal Y-ridge, needing no retouch to serve as a spear tip.")
]

