# SLOT 27: Paleolithic Art: Parietal Cave Paintings and Mobiliary Art
# ==============================================================================
s27_raw = [
    ("mcq", "Parietal Art versus Mobiliary Art Distinction",
     "In prehistoric archaeology, how is 'Parietal Art' (Cave Art) distinguished from 'Mobiliary Art' (Portable Art)?",
     "Parietal art consists of non-movable paintings, drawings, and engravings on the permanent walls and ceilings of deep caves; mobiliary art consists of small, portable carved figurines and decorated tools",
     ["Parietal art is made on modern canvas, while mobiliary art is made on stone walls",
      "Parietal art is carved out of animal bone, while mobiliary art is painted on cave ceilings",
      "There is no distinction; they are identical terms"],
     "Parietal art (from Latin paries, wall) is immovable rock/cave art; Mobiliary art (from mobilis, movable) consists of portable figurines, decorated tools, and beads."),
    ("stmt", "Altamira and Lascaux Cave Art",
     "Altamira cave in northern Spain, discovered by Marcelino Sanz de Sautuola in 1879, is celebrated as the 'Sistine Chapel of Paleolithic Art' for its polychrome ceiling bison.",
     "Lascaux cave in the Dordogne region of France features magnificent Upper Paleolithic paintings, including the famed 'Hall of the Bulls' depicting horses, deer, and giant bulls.",
     1,
     "Both statements are correct. Altamira (Spain) and Lascaux (France) represent the pinnacle of Upper Paleolithic parietal art."),
    ("ar", "Henri Breuil's Hunting Magic Hypothesis",
     "Abbé Henri Breuil proposed the 'Hunting Magic' (Sympathetic Magic) theory to explain why Paleolithic humans ventured into pitch-black cave depths to paint wild animals.",
     "Breuil argued that by painting game animals wounded by darts or spears, prehistoric hunters believed they could magically control the animals' spirits and guarantee successful real-world hunts.",
     1,
     "Both (A) and (R) are true, and (R) accurately conveys Breuil's classic sympathetic magic hypothesis (influenced by Frazer's Golden Bough)."),
    ("match", "Prehistoric Art Sites and Their World Significance",
     "Match the Paleolithic art sites in List-I with their diagnostic discoveries in List-II:",
     [("A", "Altamira (Spain)"),
      ("B", "Lascaux (France)"),
      ("C", "Chauvet Cave (France)"),
      ("D", "Bhimbetka (Madhya Pradesh, India)")],
     [("I", "Discovered in 1994; exceptional Aurignacian paintings dated to ~36,000 BP depicting dangerous carnivores (lions, rhinos, bears)"),
      ("II", "Discovered by V.S. Wakankar (1957); hundreds of painted rock shelters with continuous art from Upper Paleolithic to historic periods"),
      ("III", "Polychrome bison painted on natural rock ceiling bulges to create realistic three-dimensional relief"),
      ("IV", "Magdalenian masterpieces including the Hall of the Bulls and the mysterious 'Bird-Headed Man' scene")],
     "A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II",
     "Altamira has 3D ceiling bison (III); Lascaux has Hall of Bulls (IV); Chauvet has 36 ka carnivores (I); Bhimbetka has Indian rock art sequence (II)."),
    ("seq", "Parietal Art Antiquity Sequence",
     "Arrange the famous prehistoric cave art complexes in order of their earliest painted strata from oldest to most recent:",
     [("A", "Chauvet Cave, France (Aurignacian; dynamic paintings of lions and rhinos dated to ~36,000 BP)"),
      ("B", "Lascaux Cave, France (Early Magdalenian / Solutrean; Hall of the Bulls dated to ~17,000 BP)"),
      ("C", "Altamira Cave, Spain (Middle Magdalenian; polychrome ceiling bison dated to ~14,500 BP)"),
      ("D", "Niaux Cave, France (Late Magdalenian; Salon Noir black outline drawings dated to ~13,000 BP)")],
     "A, B, C, D",
     "Art antiquity progresses: Chauvet (~36 ka) -> Lascaux (~17 ka) -> Altamira (~14.5 ka) -> Niaux (~13 ka)."),
    ("mcq", "V.S. Wakankar and Bhimbetka Rock Art Discovery",
     "In 1957, which renowned Indian archaeologist discovered the sprawling complex of over 700 rock shelters at Bhimbetka in the Raisen district of Madhya Pradesh?",
     "Dr. Vishnu Shridhar Wakankar",
     ["H.D. Sankalia", "Sir John Marshall", "B.B. Lal"],
     "V.S. Wakankar discovered Bhimbetka in 1957 while traveling by train, recognizing rock shelters that now form a UNESCO World Heritage site of Indian rock art."),
    ("mcq", "Pigments and Techniques in Paleolithic Cave Paintings",
     "What raw mineral pigments were utilized by Upper Paleolithic cave artists to produce red, yellow, and black colors?",
     "Red and yellow ochre (iron hematite and limonite) for reds and yellows, and charcoal and manganese dioxide for blacks",
     ["Synthetic oil paints and industrial acrylic colors",
      "Crushed gold dust mixed with melted copper",
      "Vegetable fruit juices that washed off in rain"],
     "Artists pulverized natural mineral pigments: iron oxides (hematite/ochre) for vibrant reds/yellows, and manganese dioxide or charcoal for deep blacks, mixed with animal fat."),
    ("mcq", "Venus of Willendorf Carving",
     "The iconic 'Venus of Willendorf' (discovered in Austria and dated to ~25,000 BP) is a 11-cm mobiliary statuette carved from:",
     "Oolitic limestone tinted with red ochre",
     ["Pure stainless steel", "Polished white marble from Greece", "Synthetic modeling clay"],
     "The Venus of Willendorf is carved from non-local oolitic limestone and tinted with red ochre, depicting an exaggerated female form with intricate braided hair."),
    ("mcq", "André Leroi-Gourhan: Structuralist Theory of Cave Art",
     "French archaeologist André Leroi-Gourhan rejected the simplistic 'hunting magic' theory, proposing a structuralist interpretation of Paleolithic cave art wherein:",
     "Caves represent structured, sacred sanctuaries with systematic symbolic layouts, organizing male principles (horses, ibex) and female principles (bison, aurochs) in complementary zones",
     ["Caves were commercial art galleries where paintings were sold for gold coins",
      "Drawings were random scribbles made by bored children left home alone",
      "Paintings were drawn by visiting extraterrestrial astronauts"],
     "Leroi-Gourhan and Annette Laming-Emperaire demonstrated cave layouts were deliberate mythograms: animals embodied gendered binary principles distributed in specific cave chambers."),
    ("mcq", "Bhimbetka Upper Paleolithic Paintings Characteristics",
     "At Bhimbetka, the earliest Upper Paleolithic rock paintings (Period I) are characterized stylistically by:",
     "Large, energetic outline drawings of wild animals (bison, tigers, rhinoceroses, boars) in dark red and green, often with geometric infilling",
     ["Tiny colorful pictures of British steam trains",
      "Portraits of Roman emperors wearing laurel wreaths",
      "Scenes of astronauts walking on the moon"],
     "Period I (Upper Paleolithic) at Bhimbetka features massive stick-like and geometric infilled outlines of megafaunal animals (bison, boars, rhinos) executed in deep red and green.")
]

s27_extras = [
    ("Negative Hand Stencils Technique", "In Paleolithic caves such as Gargas (France) and Pech Merle, prehistoric people created striking 'Negative Hand Stencils' by:",
     "Placing a hand flat against the rock wall and spitting or blowing powdered pigment through a hollow bone pipe over the hand, leaving its unpainted silhouette",
     ["Dipping the entire hand in black ink and stamping it like a rubber stamp", "Cutting off fingers and pasting them to the rock", "Tracing the hand with a modern graphite pencil"],
     "Negative stencils were made by aerographing pigment: blowing powdered ochre through a hollow bone blowpipe over the hand pressed against the wall."),
    ("Finger Flutings (Macaroni) in Caves", "In deep, dark limestone caves like Rouffignac (France) and Koonalda (Australia), Upper Paleolithic humans created 'Finger Flutings' (macaroni) by:",
     "Dragging fingers through the soft, clay-like 'moonmilk' (hydromagnesite) deposits coating cave walls to form meandering lines and animal outlines",
     ["Writing Italian recipes on wooden boards", "Carving letters with steel knives", "Throwing soft mud pies at the ceiling"],
     "Finger flutings (meanders or macaroni) were drawn by fingertips sliding through soft moonmilk clay on cave walls in absolute darkness, illuminated by torchlight."),
    ("Lascaux 'Shaft of the Dead Man'", "The deepest, most enigmatic scene in Lascaux cave (in the Shaft) depicts an ithyphallic bird-headed man falling backwards before:",
     "A disemboweled, wounded bison bristling with a spear, next to a bird perched on a pole",
     ["A pack of lions eating an elephant", "A modern airplane flying over mountains", "A herd of domesticated cows eating grass"],
     "The Lascaux Shaft scene is an iconic Paleolithic narrative: a bird-headed man, a spear piercing a disemboweled charging bison, and a bird-topped stick (likely shamanic trance)."),
    ("Spit and Spray Aerographing", "Microscopic examination of paint droplets on the walls of Lascaux and Chauvet demonstrated that prehistoric artists invented the first 'Airbrush', using:",
     "Hollow bird bones as blowpipes or spraying mouth-held pigment slurries directly onto rock surfaces",
     ["Modern pneumatic air compressors driven by electricity", "Pressurized aerosol spray paint cans", "Steam-powered paint pumps"],
     "Cave painters used two hollow bird bones as an atomizer or blew pigment from their mouths, achieving smooth shaded gradient tones across animal flanks."),
    ("Bhimbetka Mesolithic Paintings Subject Matter", "How do the Mesolithic paintings (Period II) at Bhimbetka differ thematically from the earlier Upper Paleolithic paintings?",
     "They depict dynamic scenes of daily community life: communal group hunts with bows and arrows, honey gathering, dancing in lines, and child-rearing",
     ["They depict medieval tank warfare and trench combat", "They show Christian cathedrals and church organs", "They depict modern cricket and soccer matches"],
     "Mesolithic rock art at Bhimbetka shifts focus to dynamic human culture: men armed with bows and arrows hunting deer, group dances, family domestic life, and honey-gathering."),
    ("Natural Rock Contours in Cave Art", "A remarkable aesthetic technique of Paleolithic cave painters at Altamira and Pech Merle was:",
     "Utilizing natural rock swellings, bulges, and crevices on cave walls to represent the muscular humps, bellies, and contours of painted animals, creating a 3D effect",
     ["Chiseling the entire cave wall until it was completely flat like modern drywall", "Painting only on smooth wooden panels glued to the cave", "Covering the cave ceiling with wallpaper"],
     "Prehistoric painters were master visualizers: natural calcite bulges were chosen to depict the muscular shoulder hump of a bison or the rump of a wild horse in relief."),
    ("Superimposition of Paintings in Rock Art", "In rock art shelters like Bhimbetka, archaeologists use 'Superimposition' (paintings layered over older paintings) to:",
     "Establish the relative chronological sequence of artistic styles and periods, as the layer on top is younger than the layer beneath it",
     ["Clean away old paintings to make the rock look brand new", "Measure the temperature of the rock shelter", "Count the number of tourists who visited the cave"],
     "Superimposition provides relative stratigraphy for rock art: analyzing which pigments overlap reveals the chronological evolution of artistic styles."),
    ("Art for Art's Sake Theory (Lartet and Christy)", "The 19th-century 'Art for Art's Sake' theory (L'art pour l'art) proposed by Édouard Lartet suggested that Paleolithic art was produced simply because:",
     "Humans possessed an inherent, spontaneous aesthetic impulse and abundant leisure time during food-rich seasons to decorate their surroundings",
     ["Humans were paid salaries by royal art academies", "Priests threatened to execute anyone who refused to paint", "Artists wanted to sell paintings to modern tourists"],
     "Lartet and Christy initially believed ice-age hunters had leisure time and natural creative drive, painting simply for aesthetic pleasure, though later superseded by symbolic theories."),
    ("Shamanism Theory of Cave Art (Lewis-Williams and Clottes)", "In 'The Shamans of Prehistory' (1996), David Lewis-Williams and Jean Clottes proposed the Neuropsychological / Shamanic model, arguing that:",
     "Cave art originated from altered states of consciousness (trances) experienced by shamans, who entered deep caves (the spirit underworld) to fix their hallucinations on the rock wall",
     ["Paintings were drawn by blind people who could not see colors", "Cave art was drawn by cave bears playing with mud", "Paintings were maps showing oil and gas reserves"],
     "Lewis-Williams argued that entoptic phenomena and animal spirit visions in trance states were perceived on cave walls (the membrane to the underworld) and traced by shamans."),
    ("Koonalda Cave (Australia) Finger Markings", "Koonalda Cave on the Nullarbor Plain of South Australia contains extensive fluted finger markings and geometric engravings executed in absolute darkness:",
     "Dating back over 20,000 years, demonstrating that Australian Aboriginal symbolic cave traditions were contemporaneous with European Upper Paleolithic art",
     ["Painted last year by Australian school children", "Made by modern sheep scratching their wool against the rock", "Created by European gold miners in 1850"],
     "Koonalda Cave features thousands of square meters of finger flutings deep in underground limestone caverns, proving ancient symbolic traditions among Pleistocene First Australians.")
]

